<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ikeda Terumasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1565">1565</a>–<a href="/years/1613">1613</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ikeda Terumasa was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> who fought for him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>. Rewarded with Harima province and 520,000 koku, he rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himeji-castle">Himeji Castle</a> between 1601 and 1609 into the great white keep that survives today, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ikeda-terumasa.jpg" alt="Ikeda Terumasa" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terumasa_Ikeda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ikeda Terumasa was born on 31 January 1565, the second son and heir of Ikeda Tsuneoki, also known as Ikeda Nobuteru; his childhood name was Araokojimaru. He held Ikejiri Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mino-province">Mino Province</a>. At the siege of Hanakuma castle in 1580 he personally killed six enemies, and for his exploits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> sent him a letter of commendation. At the Battle of Nagakute in 1584 his father and Mori Nagayoshi were both killed, and Terumasa, persuaded by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, retreated as the Tokugawa forces won.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanto-region">Kanto region</a> in 1590, Terumasa was granted Yoshida in Mikawa, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of 152,000 koku. During the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> that began in 1592 he stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, guarding the east, building large ships, carrying provisions to Nagoya Castle and working on Fushimi Castle. In 1594, with Hideyoshi's mediation, he married Tokuhime, a daughter of Ieyasu, and after Hideyoshi's death in 1598 the Ikeda drifted into Ieyasu's camp.</p><p>That year he was one of the seven generals who confronted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>, an incident long told as a plot to kill him; modern historians such as Watanabe Daimon see it rather as a legal conflict. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> campaign began in 1600 Terumasa sided at once with his father-in-law and persuaded Nakagawa Hidenari to do the same. He led the Eastern Army across the river from Kawada, routed Oda Hidenobu at Yoneno and attacked Takegahana Castle. At Sekigahara he commanded 4,560 troops in the rear guard and afterwards pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-morichika">Chosokabe Morichika</a>, who escaped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bizen was added to his lands in 1603, and he assigned it to his eldest son, Toshitaka. He was known as saigoku no shōgun, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">Shōgun</a> of Western Japan. By his death on 16 March 1613 the Ikeda ruled Harima, Bizen, Inaba and Awaji, with a combined income of around 1,000,000 koku; after Toshitaka's death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Bakufu</a> reduced the family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a>. His sword Ōkanehira, a tachi forged by Kanehira of the Ko-Bizen school in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a>, is a National Treasure in the Tokyo National Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ikeda Terumasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Battle of Sekigahara in 1600, Tokugawa Ieyasu granted the castle to his son-in-law <b>Ikeda Terumasa</b> as a reward for his help in the battle.</blockquote><a href="/work/himeji-castle">Himeji Castle · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After Sekigahara, Tokugawa Ieyasu gave Harima to his son-in-law <b>Ikeda Terumasa</b> with 520,000 koku in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/state/himeji-domain">Himeji Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiyosu">Kiyosu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Himeji Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.21,136.85&amp;year=1596" aria-label="Kiyosu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ikeda Terumasa"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4089 4502h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4089 4502h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.21,136.85&amp;year=1596">What was here in 1596? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
